                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "That was short but really impressive! I adore things that make me think, although recently I've been playing only relaxing games. It was a nice change, thank you for this!\n\n The whole game seems to be so unique. I liked the narration from different points of view. I managed to experience such a great variety of emotions. Some twists of the plot were a real surprise. Some ideas I couldn't easily accept and kept arguing while reading. Some moments were so funny I even forgot all that gloomy atmosphere. And at the end of the game, despite all my arguments, I managed maybe not to accept but understand what you meant. The ending turned out to be even slightly encouraging!:)\n\nBy the way, it's the second time I use “you” because this work appears to be so personal. The game is made by one person. Reading the afterword you can't but feel a great respect to this man for creating this decent work! I sincerely wish you good luck with all your creative projects, especially visual novels ;)! \n\nTraditionally some negativity at the end... Sad but I start getting used to the language mistakes. But what was really irritating in this game is free space after apostrophes. This game was the first when I tried autoplay and in the fastest mode. That was a great feeling to lie with my hands behind the head and enjoy the story with no need to constantly click my mouse. Why haven't I tried it before? But!!!!! that space constantly created the illusion of a new word, that was so confusing, especially with that fast speed!!!!!!! And one last tiny complaint!!!!!! Maybe you don't need so many exclamatory marks?! :)\n\n[table]\n[tr]\n[th]Find more in a [url=https://steamcommunity.com/groups/readingnook] Reading Nook [/url][/th]\n[/tr]\n[/table]",
